Grill one section at a time, right after drafting it. Ask at most 3 questions per round, each with
options and a recommended default, and challenge every requirement of the section:

- **Measurable?** It has a number, a yes/no rule anyone can check, or is marked as verifiable only
  in operation. Bare adjectives (fast, scalable, secure, robust, intuitive, seamless,
  user-friendly) fail unless a measure follows.
- **Decision-relevant?** Removing it would change an implementation choice or a check. If not,
  drop it.
- **Long-lived?** It stays true after a refactor, a rename or a library swap. Names of files,
  classes or library versions fail this test; name a technology only when it is a constraint.
- **Sourced?** A number you proposed (`Source: proposed`) needs the user's explicit confirmation.
  Never keep a guessed number.
- **Conflicting?** Two applicable requirements that pull against each other (latency against cost,
  privacy against observability) get an explicit trade-off the user chooses.
- **Affordable?** Name what a strict target costs (infrastructure, complexity, time) so the user can
  relax it on purpose.

Stop when the user accepts the section. A question the user defers goes into the brief as an open
item, never into the document as a requirement.
